WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.560
"The Side Effects
of Burning Incense"

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.080
Frankincense and myrrh date
back to thousands of years

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.000
before the three wise men.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.680
The burning of witch's incense

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.000
may have actually had
a hygienic functions,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.560
like maybe repelling mosquitoes,
and when put to the test,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.480
incense fumes were able to kill off

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.640
some bacteria and mold in the air

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.920
but may also carry health risks.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.880
Although the adverse health effects
of second-hand tobacco smoke

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:38.160
are well recognized, the impact
of burning incense in the home

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.600
has received less attention, but
burning incense has been found

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.200
to generate like four times the
particulate matter as cigarettes;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.680
so, incense may be even worse!

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.360
No wonder home incense use
may have significant adverse

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.440
health effects, particularly
on the heart and lungs,

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.840
including childhood asthma.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.960
No wonder, since the incense
smoke particle size

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.520
peaks down in the danger range,
so ultrafine they can float

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.560
down into the deepest
parts of the lungs.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.200
It’s not just the little ash
particles in the smoke though,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.040
there’s carbon monoxide, nitric
oxides, sulfur dioxide,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.240
carcinogenic polycyclic
aromatic hydrocarbons,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:20.560
and volatile organic compounds
like benzene and formaldehyde.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.240
The World Health Organization suggests
limiting indoor formaldehyde

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.840
to about 80 parts per billion,
but even with a window open

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.400
during the hour or so
incense is burning,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.932
formaldehyde levels
exceed the safety limit.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
What does this all mean in
terms of disease outcomes?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.080
Studies on rats show incense can
do all sorts of terrible things,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.640
but what about people?

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.000
A study of thousands of children
followed over time found

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.200
that exposure to household
incense burning was associated

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.520
with impaired lung function,
reduced lung function growth,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.000
and increased risks of
respiratory diseases and symptoms.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.720
Daily exposure is associated
with impaired lung function

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.400
in adolescents too, though
interestingly those who had

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.520
pets at home appeared to
have better lung function.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.200
Something I noted previously, how
having a dog or cat in the house

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.600
during early life may protect against

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.080
childhood asthma and allergy.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.680
What about the heart?

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.640
Long-term exposure to incense
burning in the home environment

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.800
was associated with an increased
risk of cardiovascular mortality.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.280
Approximately 8 percent of
heart disease deaths and

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.720
12 percent of stroke deaths could
potentially be attributed

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.120
to incense use, though they were
looking at long-term exposure,

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.199
so like incense burning at home on
a daily basis for more than 20 years.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.880
What about cancer?

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.400
Temple workers exposed to incense
day in and day out were found

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.600
to have evidence of
signiﬁcantly more DNA damage,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.400
including DNA strand breaks.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.080
Does this translate
into greater cancer risk?

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:02.560
One unsolved mystery has been
why non-smoking Asian women

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.640
have such high lung cancer rates.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.960
Might it be incense?

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.160
Probably not, since the
association between incense use

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.720
and lung cancer
remains inconclusive,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.520
though incense use does
appear to be linked to cancers

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.680
of the upper respiratory tract,
as well as brain tumors

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.000
among children whose mother
were exposed to incense.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.440
Three times the odds, even more
than that of second-hand smoke.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.520
In fact, even more than the
consumption of bologna sandwiches.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.000
Processed meat consumption
only appears to at most

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.360
double brain tumor
risk among children.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.880
Even without tumors, a study of
15,000 infants found that

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.920
household incense burning
was associated with a delay

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:48.092
in brain development milestones
such as when they start to walk.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.240
And then on the other
side of the life cycle,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.600
incense exposure among older adults
was associated with reduced

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.320
cognitive performance and adverse
structural changes in the brain.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.600
The researchers conclude that
this calls for safer practices,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.160
such as avoiding
burning incense indoors

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:07.440
or using safer incense alternatives.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.400
So-called environmentally friendly
types of incense have been

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.960
put to the test, and…

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.760
they were found to contain
even higher concentrations

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:20.640
of several potentially carcinogenic
polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.520
Despite being a
recognized health hazard,

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.720
burning incense remains
in widespread use.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.320
In the very least, we may want to
avoid burning incense in the home

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.880
when susceptible
individuals are present,

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.840
such as the very young,
the very old, or

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.480
people with a family history of
allergy or pre-existing

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.320
cardiovascular or respiratory disease.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.160
One innovative suggestion
is electronic incense,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.280
like the e-cigarette
of the incense world,

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:50.960
so you can get the fragrance

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.240
without the combustion
by-products of smoke.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.080
Most of the concern about
e-cigarette-related adverse events

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.400
traditionally have revolved
around the nicotine,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.560
which wouldn’t be a
problem with e-incense,

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.960
the concern that e-cigarette use
would lead to real cigarette use.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.960
But now that there have been
thousands of cases of lung injury

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.520
associated with vaping, and
we’re still actually not sure

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.600
what’s causing the issue,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.760
it seems to me we
should figure that out

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.240
before we push for the
electronic incense equivalent.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.120
One option in the meantime
is scented candles,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.760
which, under normal
conditions of use,

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.655
do not appear to pose known
health risks to the consumer.

